1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,648
Here's a number worth sitting with before anything else in this lecture.

2
00:00:04,648 --> 00:00:09,544
A batch of twenty statics, the kind Section 3 walked you through generating, costs

3
00:00:09,544 --> 00:00:12,887
somewhere around a dollar or two in raw generation fees.

4
00:00:12,887 --> 00:00:17,543
A single day of Section 7's testing architecture, eight ad sets at twenty-five

5
00:00:17,543 --> 00:00:19,454
dollars each, costs two hundred.

6
00:00:19,454 --> 00:00:21,424
That gap is not a rounding error.

7
00:00:21,424 --> 00:00:23,096
Generation is close to free.

8
00:00:23,096 --> 00:00:26,976
Media spend is the real cost this whole system exists to protect.

9
00:00:26,976 --> 00:00:31,573
Worrying about generation cost the way you'd worry about media spend gets the

10
00:00:31,573 --> 00:00:36,349
proportions backwards, and it's an easy mistake to make, because both show up as

11
00:00:36,349 --> 00:00:37,662
numbers on an invoice.

12
00:00:37,662 --> 00:00:42,677
The honest question isn't "can I afford to generate a big batch." At current prices,

13
00:00:42,677 --> 00:00:43,752
almost always yes.

14
00:00:43,752 --> 00:00:48,289
It's "can I afford to properly test what I generate," which is a media-spend

15
00:00:48,289 --> 00:00:53,124
question, not a generation-spend one, and it's the question this lecture actually

16
00:00:53,124 --> 00:00:53,602
answers.

17
00:00:53,602 --> 00:00:56,050
Why does this confusion happen so easily?

18
00:00:56,050 --> 00:01:01,005
Because generation is the part you actively do, click a button, wait a few seconds,

19
00:01:01,005 --> 00:01:05,721
watch a result appear, while media spend just quietly accrues in the background

20
00:01:05,721 --> 00:01:07,154
across a whole campaign.

21
00:01:07,154 --> 00:01:09,601
The visible cost feels like the real one.

22
00:01:09,601 --> 00:01:14,258
The one compounding silently in an ad account is where your actual money goes.

23
00:01:14,808 --> 00:01:18,260
Read down that table and notice where the real money sits.

24
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:22,962
Generating even a large batch, statics and video variants together, lands under

25
00:01:22,962 --> 00:01:24,153
fifty dollars total.

26
00:01:24,153 --> 00:01:29,153
Testing that batch properly, even at Harlan's modest scale, costs four times that in

27
00:01:29,153 --> 00:01:29,927
a single day.

28
00:01:29,927 --> 00:01:34,807
This is why Section 1's volume maths held up economically, not just statistically.

29
00:01:34,807 --> 00:01:39,629
Generating more variants to test barely moves your total cost, because generation

30
00:01:39,629 --> 00:01:41,355
was never the expensive part.

31
00:01:41,355 --> 00:01:46,235
Media spend was always the expensive part, which is why Section 7's kill rules and

32
00:01:46,235 --> 00:01:50,164
CAC ceiling matter so much more than anything about the generator.

33
00:01:50,164 --> 00:01:53,259
Once an angle actually wins, the gap widens further.

34
00:01:53,259 --> 00:01:58,199
Harlan's winning angle now runs at four hundred dollars a day, a real core-campaign

35
00:01:58,199 --> 00:02:02,961
budget, while the generation cost that produced the winning cut was a handful of

36
00:02:02,961 --> 00:02:04,866
dollars, spent once, months ago.

37
00:02:04,866 --> 00:02:08,556
The return on that original spend, at this point, is enormous.

38
00:02:08,556 --> 00:02:12,187
Hold both figures from that bottom row together for a moment.

39
00:02:12,187 --> 00:02:17,127
A core-campaign budget of four hundred dollars a day is only defensible because the

40
00:02:17,127 --> 00:02:21,829
twenty-one-dollar CAC underneath it is real, tested, proven against Section 8's

41
00:02:21,829 --> 00:02:26,413
decision tree, not assumed from a feeling about which creative "should" work.

42
00:02:26,413 --> 00:02:29,091
The generation cost that got here is trivial.

43
00:02:29,091 --> 00:02:33,793
The discipline that turned a cheap batch into a budget worth scaling to is not.

44
00:02:34,343 --> 00:02:37,148
Five lines, and the entire point is line five.

45
00:02:37,148 --> 00:02:41,905
For almost every account at almost any real scale, that ratio comes out large,

46
00:02:41,905 --> 00:02:47,027
meaning one day of testing costs many times more than the entire batch that made the

47
00:02:47,027 --> 00:02:47,881
test possible.

48
00:02:47,881 --> 00:02:52,394
Fill in your own numbers rather than borrowing Harlan's or this lecture's.

49
00:02:52,394 --> 00:02:57,211
Generation pricing moves quickly, tool to tool and month to month, and whatever

50
00:02:57,211 --> 00:03:02,333
specific price you pay today is worth checking again before you trust this model six

51
00:03:02,333 --> 00:03:03,309
months from now.

52
00:03:03,309 --> 00:03:05,382
Notice what this model is not for.

53
00:03:05,382 --> 00:03:10,444
It won't tell you whether to spend more on testing, that's Section 7's job, decided

54
00:03:10,444 --> 00:03:14,225
from your margin, not from how cheap generation happens to be.

55
00:03:14,225 --> 00:03:19,286
It only tells you where your actual cost pressure sits, so you stop rationing batch

56
00:03:19,286 --> 00:03:24,408
size to save pennies while the real budget decision happens somewhere else entirely.

57
00:03:24,408 --> 00:03:27,640
Line three deserves a second look before you move on.

58
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:32,519
It's tempting to round it to zero once you've compared it against line four, and

59
00:03:32,519 --> 00:03:35,080
that temptation is itself worth resisting.

60
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:39,897
A batch that costs almost nothing still costs something, and tracking that real

61
00:03:39,897 --> 00:03:45,020
number, however small, is what keeps this whole model honest rather than becoming an

62
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:47,947
excuse to stop counting generation spend at all.

63
00:03:48,497 --> 00:03:53,430
One honest trap worth naming, because cheap generation makes it genuinely tempting.

64
00:03:53,430 --> 00:03:58,186
If a static costs eight cents and a video clip costs a few dollars, it's easy to

65
00:03:58,186 --> 00:04:02,406
reason that a mistake barely matters, you'll just generate another one.

66
00:04:02,406 --> 00:04:04,962
That's true for the generation cost itself.

67
00:04:04,962 --> 00:04:09,777
It is not true for what happens if the mistake ships before Section 6's checklist

68
00:04:09,777 --> 00:04:10,431
catches it.

69
00:04:10,431 --> 00:04:15,187
A claim that oversteps the boundary, or a licence gap on an avatar, costs you in

70
00:04:15,187 --> 00:04:18,872
policy strikes and wasted media spend, not in generation fees.

71
00:04:18,872 --> 00:04:23,687
Keep the pre-flight discipline exactly as tight as this course already taught it,

72
00:04:23,687 --> 00:04:26,243
regardless of how cheap the generator gets.

73
00:04:26,243 --> 00:04:29,275
Cheap generation is a reason to test more variants.

74
00:04:29,275 --> 00:04:34,208
It is never a reason to check them less carefully before they spend a single dollar

75
00:04:34,208 --> 00:04:35,100
of real budget.

76
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:40,093
Would you skip a safety check on something expensive just because replacing it would

77
00:04:40,093 --> 00:04:40,628
be cheap?

78
00:04:40,628 --> 00:04:42,828
Framed that way, the trap is obvious.

79
00:04:42,828 --> 00:04:47,524
It's only the cheapness of generation that makes it feel like an exception, and

80
00:04:47,524 --> 00:04:52,398
nothing about a claim, a rights gap, or a missing disclosure caption actually gets

81
00:04:52,398 --> 00:04:57,391
cheaper to fix once it's already spending real media budget against a live audience.

82
00:04:57,941 --> 00:05:03,095
Next lecture asks a different question about the same economics: who should actually

83
00:05:03,095 --> 00:05:08,003
be doing this work, given how little of the real cost sits in generation and how

84
00:05:08,003 --> 00:05:10,028
much sits in disciplined testing.

85
00:05:10,028 --> 00:05:14,875
Before that, run your own numbers through this lecture's five-line model, using

86
00:05:14,875 --> 00:05:17,820
today's actual prices, not the ones quoted here.

87
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:22,973
Prices in this category move fast, and a stale number is worse than no number at all

88
00:05:22,973 --> 00:05:24,568
if you end up trusting it.

89
00:05:24,568 --> 00:05:29,354
Hold onto the ratio you calculate, not just the raw numbers either side of it.

90
00:05:29,354 --> 00:05:34,508
A ratio of fifty to one tells a different story than a ratio of five to one, even if

91
00:05:34,508 --> 00:05:37,330
both accounts spend roughly the same on media.

92
00:05:37,330 --> 00:05:42,299
The lower the ratio, the closer generation cost actually is to mattering, and for

93
00:05:42,299 --> 00:05:47,208
most accounts running current tools, that ratio sits high enough that generation

94
00:05:47,208 --> 00:05:49,110
genuinely isn't the constraint.

95
00:05:49,110 --> 00:05:53,834
Check your own, though, rather than assuming that holds for you specifically.

96
00:05:53,834 --> 00:05:58,926
Tool pricing and account scale both vary enough that the honest number is worth the

97
00:05:58,926 --> 00:06:01,012
two minutes it takes to calculate.
